    "collected_at": "2026-10-21T14:50:39.199748+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/this-1970-pontiac-has-the-perfect-package-all-original-survivor-in-a-garage-since-day-one-277306.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/this-1970-pontiac-has-the-perfect-package-all-original-survivor-in-a-garage-since-day-one-277306.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-10-21T13:00:01+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-21T13:00:01+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-21T15:25:17.951961+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-21T15:25:17.951961+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nThis 1970 Pontiac Has the Perfect Package: All-Original Survivor, in a Garage Since Day One\n14 photos\nPhoto: eBay seller risingsunset\nWhile 1970 was in some ways the beginning of the end for the GTO, this Catalina is here to remind us that Pontiac was still fine that year. This car is advertised as an all-original survivor, and the reason it’s online is simple: it needs a loving home where it can get the minor TLC it needs.\nThis isn’t a restored or molested car. It’s a specimen that has always been babied, and the seller claims that this Pontiac never slept outside. It has always been parked inside since day one, which kind of explains its condition.\nThe owner says the Catalina “needs absolutely nothing,” but I don’t think this is entirely true. It does need a few occasional touches, especially if your objective is to park the car in a collection. Just open the trunk to see what I mean.\nThere’s obvious surface rust, but there’s nothing too dramatic or something that could make people walk away. It’s minor corrosion that hasn’t gotten through, so my recommendation is that you call the seller and arrange an in-person inspection before you even make an offer. This Catalina isn’t supposed to be a car that you drive every day, so yes, you must make sure that it checks all the right boxes.\nPhoto: eBay seller risingsunset The Catalina went through lots of changes in the second half of the ‘60s, including the demise of the 2+2 in 1968 and the introduction of larger and larger grilles. While 1970 models aren’t exactly rare - if you want a rare Catalina, you should look for a last-year 2+2, as Pontiac produced only 1,768 coupes and convertibles in 1967- they are still highly desirable, particularly thanks to their design.\nPontiac itself bragged about the redesigned Catalina in 1970.\n“Face it, folks. The 1970 Catalina is all wrong. For the money, you should get a so-so-sized car with hee-haw styling and a ho-hum interior. But, no. Catalina is big, big, big. Lots of room for legs and heads,” Pontiac said before moving its attention to engines and styling. The grille was the thing that caught everybody’s attention.\n“The styling - Catalina’s strength. That incredible new grille is strictly top-of-the-line,” Pontiac said.\nThe GM brand used every occasion to recommend that buyers pay extra for the Ventura package, which brought carpeted lower door panels, new seats with extra padding, a perforated taffeta headliner, and special trim inside and out.\nPhoto: eBay seller risingsunset The Catalina sedan was standard with a 350ci engine rated at 255 horsepower, while the rest of the versions were fitted with a 400ci unit with 290 horsepower. Pontiac offered not one, not two, but five optional engines at a time when the American car space was moving towards more efficient and smaller engines.\nThe optional engine list started with a 400ci unit with 265 horsepower and went all the way up to 455 cubic inches and 370 horsepower.\nThis Catalina has a 400 under the hood, but that’s where the available information ends. No further specifics were shared, but considering it’s an all-original survivor, this should be the factory-installed unit.\nIt should be in tip-top shape already, considering the owner says you can drive the Pontiac home. However, I would still ask my mechanic to inspect it thoroughly, especially considering the survivor promise, which implies that the engine has never been rebuilt.\nPhoto: eBay seller risingsunset Otherwise, an in-person inspection is the one that should help figure out the full story. The paint has lost its shine, and the interior faces some discoloration, which makes perfect sense, considering the car has never been refreshed.\nThe trunk rust makes me wonder if the floors are clean, so if you go there in person, make sure you raise this Pontiac on a lift to inspect the underside. The vehicle shouldn’t come with any patches, considering it’s an all-original specimen, and the seller insists on the “no major rust anywhere” claim.\nOverall, I do think that this Catalina will find a home eventually, but I don’t think it’ll happen without further specifics and maybe a walkaround video to help bidders figure out what they’re buying.\nPhoto: eBay seller risingsunset The owner looks committed to letting the car go, as they posted this Pontiac for auction on eBay without a reserve. This allows the top bidder to take the Pontiac home when the battle ends, though if someone wants to get their hands on it without a fight, they can just trigger the Buy It Now option and pay $10,000.\nOtherwise, you can bid against others for the car, and the starting bid is $5,000. So far, the auction hasn’t received any bids, but this isn’t necessarily surprising for a no-reserve auction, as many bidders might be waiting until the last minute to submit an offer to avoid a fierce battle.\nThe vehicle is parked in Scranton, Pennsylvania, and remember that the auction ends in just 3 days, so you might be running out of time to call the seller and ask your questions.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nCatalina Pontiac Pontiac Catalina 1970 Catalina\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2028 BMW iX7 Spotted With Production Split Headlights",
